WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.460 --> 00:00:10.040
SIERRA: In 2020 five tech giants - Apple,
Amazon, Google, Microsoft, and Facebook, had

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:13.230
a combined revenue of more than $1.2 trillion.

00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:19.350
It makes sense though right - like, I for
one spent a lot of time on my computer that

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:20.420
year.

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:25.869
But many were outraged to learn how little
they had paid in taxes.

00:00:25.869 --> 00:00:29.189
This highlights a story that has been unfolding
for decades.

00:00:29.189 --> 00:00:35.270
As silicon valley moved fast and broke things,
they also got really good at finding ways

00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:41.260
to pay the lowest tax possible - which sometimes
is no tax.

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:45.980
Many of them moved to countries with lower
tax rates resulting in a race to the bottom

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:51.010
- countries competing with each other to offer
lower and lower rates.

00:00:51.010 --> 00:00:52.780
The result?

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:57.250
Hundreds of billions in lost tax revenue around
the world.

00:00:57.250 --> 00:01:01.480
Large corporations always seem to be two steps
ahead in the tax game.

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:07.290
But there may be a surprising change in store
- an international proposal to charge companies

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:10.650
the same tax rate, no matter where they go.

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:17.080
I’m Gabrielle Sierra and this is Why It
Matters, today, a global minimum tax - the

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:28.100
solution that could change it all, and the
long road ahead.

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:33.659
SIERRA: Can you tell me about this new idea
called global minimum tax?

00:01:33.659 --> 00:01:34.659
What is it?

00:01:34.659 --> 00:01:35.659
How does it work?

00:01:35.659 --> 00:01:39.410
OEI: Generally, a global minimum tax is exactly
what it says.

00:01:39.410 --> 00:01:40.869
SIERRA: This is Shuyi Oei.

00:01:40.869 --> 00:01:46.340
She is a professor at Boston College Law School
and teaches students all about the wide world

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:47.670
of tax law.

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:51.549
OEI: It's a minimum tax on corporations.

00:01:51.549 --> 00:01:55.920
And countries can tax more, but if they tax
less, then things happen.

00:01:55.920 --> 00:02:00.860
RUBIN: For several years now, the US and more
than 100 other countries have been talking

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:07.090
to try to find a way to have a more coordinated
corporate income tax system around the world.

00:02:07.090 --> 00:02:09.229
SIERRA: And this is Richard Rubin.

00:02:09.229 --> 00:02:12.920
He’s the US tax policy reporter for the
Wall Street Journal.

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:19.800
RUBIN: The idea being that companies would
have to pay 15% of their profits in taxes

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:25.650
in every country in which they operate, such
that the location of where those profits are

00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:27.200
is less important.

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:33.370
OEI: The way the current global minimum tax
design is being talked about is that there's

00:02:33.370 --> 00:02:40.550
a 15% global minimum, and by a series of rules,
if a country does not put their tax rate at

00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:46.670
15%, there's a very complicated mechanism
by which other countries can top up and tax

00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:47.670
the difference.

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:51.640
And so then the idea would be, well, the US
doesn't want some other country to tax our

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:53.120
multinationals on the difference.

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:57.019
So it's in our incentive to make our tax 15%.

00:02:57.019 --> 00:03:05.450
SIERRA: Okay let’s break it down - Tax rates
vary by location, which is why corporations

00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:09.980
will look around the globe and try to find
countries with lower tax rates.

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:15.269
Once they do, they will shift a headquarters,
a portion of their operations, or their intellectual

00:03:15.269 --> 00:03:17.860
property to that location.

00:03:17.860 --> 00:03:23.160
And of course, this is much easier for a tech
or pharmaceutical company than an old school

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:24.569
brick-and-mortar business.

00:03:24.569 --> 00:03:30.220
I mean think about - at one point companies
were mostly creating physical goods, and it

00:03:30.220 --> 00:03:35.319
was extremely difficult to move large machinery
and operations across oceans.

00:03:35.319 --> 00:03:41.319
But, in this new era, countries with favorable
tax rates are places like Ireland, Switzerland,

00:03:41.319 --> 00:03:45.140
Singapore, the Netherlands, Luxembourg, or
Hong Kong.

00:03:45.140 --> 00:03:51.390
A global minimum tax would stop this from
occurring, establishing one universal floor

00:03:51.390 --> 00:03:53.230
at 15%.

00:03:53.230 --> 00:03:59.590
If one country does not adopt a 15% corporate
tax rate, but something lower like 5%, then

00:03:59.590 --> 00:04:04.450
other countries where the company operates,
could charge the difference - meaning the

00:04:04.450 --> 00:04:07.900
company would be paying the minimum no matter
where they went.

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:15.269
RUBIN: The idea is to minimize those gaps
to reduce significantly the benefits of the

00:04:15.269 --> 00:04:17.959
profit shifting that companies can do.

00:04:17.959 --> 00:04:23.810
SIERRA: Okay so I know it doesn’t always
work out exactly this way, but in theory can

00:04:23.810 --> 00:04:28.450
you just tell me how companies are technically
supposed to be taxed?

00:04:28.450 --> 00:04:31.169
RUBIN: Companies are taxed based on their
profits.

00:04:31.169 --> 00:04:35.990
So, as opposed to you, you're taxed on your
income, you get some deductions, but we don't

00:04:35.990 --> 00:04:39.850
think of individuals as profit making in the
same way.

00:04:39.850 --> 00:04:42.340
For companies, they're taxed on their profits.

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:46.180
So, they take the revenue minus their expenses.

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:53.020
Those expenses can be wages, those expenses
can be office space, those expenses can be

00:04:53.020 --> 00:04:56.800
the construction of new facilities, all those
things that companies do.

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:03.440
And they're taxed on the profit, the money
that they make in a given year.

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:09.680
The basic principle has been you're taxed
where value is created, where profit is happening.

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:16.090
That's not always a simple thing to translate
into reality, into actual dollars.

00:05:16.090 --> 00:05:20.400
What's been driving a lot of the concern lately
is that was a relatively easy to definition

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:26.600
to think about 50 to 60 years ago, when you
could say, "Okay, well, the automobile factory

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:31.460
is in this particular place and this particular
country, therefore that's the country that

00:05:31.460 --> 00:05:35.180
should get to tax that income that that factory
is producing."

00:05:35.180 --> 00:05:40.940
But as the economy has become less tangible,
as the things that are generating profits

00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:46.210
are not factories, they're patents, they're
trademarks, they're websites that aren't in

00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:53.120
any particular place, figuring out where those
profits are has become much more difficult

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:54.440
for governments.

00:05:54.440 --> 00:05:59.520
SIERRA: Is there a global IRS that collects
from companies that operate around the world?

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:05.050
OEI: There is no global IRS in the sense that
there isn't one central organization that's

00:06:05.050 --> 00:06:10.819
going to sort of come knocking on the multinational
companies door if they don't pay the taxes.

00:06:10.819 --> 00:06:15.789
Sometimes you'll hear people say that in recent
years, the OECD has acted as a de facto world

00:06:15.789 --> 00:06:17.330
tax organization.

00:06:17.330 --> 00:06:23.509
SIERRA: The OECD, or Organisation for Economic
Co-operation and Development, has steered

00:06:23.509 --> 00:06:24.919
global tax talks.

00:06:24.919 --> 00:06:31.900
And last October, nearly 140 countries signed
onto a tentative deal, but that doesn’t

00:06:31.900 --> 00:06:33.220
mean it’s across the finish line.

00:06:33.220 --> 00:06:38.800
For it to work, all signatories have to go
home and get it approved through their legislative

00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:40.880
bodies as well.

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:45.979
Implementation is complicated and the details
are still being ironed out - what is the compatibility

00:06:45.979 --> 00:06:50.440
with current domestic tax laws? and what needs
to change?

00:06:50.440 --> 00:06:56.550
This question signals a bigger issue - there
are two political battles - one on the international

00:06:56.550 --> 00:06:59.840
stage and one in each individual country.

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:06.819
OEI: The OECD has taken probably the most
active role in the international space.

00:07:06.819 --> 00:07:12.289
It's making policy, setting norms and standards,
putting out models, and then countries adopt

00:07:12.289 --> 00:07:13.289
that.

00:07:13.289 --> 00:07:15.870
But there is no central global tax authority.

00:07:15.870 --> 00:07:22.199
That's generally a matter of domestic law
and sovereignty of individual nation states.

00:07:22.199 --> 00:07:31.370
SIERRA: Okay so in other words, new technology,
IPs, all that, have given companies more flexibility

00:07:31.370 --> 00:07:33.530
on where they are based.

00:07:33.530 --> 00:07:39.620
And as companies exercise this new flexibility,
there is no centralized oversight.

00:07:39.620 --> 00:07:43.960
This makes it harder to tax corporations as
they shift profits around.

00:07:43.960 --> 00:07:49.840
Let’s talk about why some countries are
offering extremely low tax rates in the first

00:07:49.840 --> 00:07:50.840
place.

00:07:50.840 --> 00:07:54.380
OEI: Tax is one of the metrics along which
countries compete.

00:07:54.380 --> 00:07:58.620
And so what the global minimum taxes is trying
to solve is that race to the bottom.

00:07:58.620 --> 00:08:02.840
And so the race to the bottom is countries
have an incentive to drive tax rates lower

00:08:02.840 --> 00:08:06.669
and lower in order to attract taxpayers to
residence in their jurisdiction.

00:08:06.669 --> 00:08:07.699
SIERRA: Interesting.

00:08:07.699 --> 00:08:13.259
OEI: The details are mind bogglingly terrible,
but the bare bones idea is that.

00:08:13.259 --> 00:08:18.129
SIERRA: Basically countries want big companies
to reside in their borders.

00:08:18.129 --> 00:08:22.659
This isn’t just for bragging rights, but
also because it could provide jobs and promote

00:08:22.659 --> 00:08:24.460
local economic growth.

00:08:24.460 --> 00:08:29.909
So to attract a big company, a country will
offer a low corporate tax rate.

00:08:29.909 --> 00:08:33.320
And another country might offer them an even
lower rate.

00:08:33.320 --> 00:08:39.860
And another one… you see where I am going
with this - over time countries will go lower

00:08:39.860 --> 00:08:45.279
and lower to attract big companies to their
shores, which creates that race to the bottom

00:08:45.279 --> 00:08:49.570
and allows companies to pay less and less
taxes as they go.

00:08:49.570 --> 00:08:55.339
And of course this is just one of many ways
for corporations to legally work the tax system.

00:08:55.339 --> 00:09:00.930
There are other ways they do it too, let’s
look specifically at the US for a moment.

00:09:00.930 --> 00:09:07.600
OEI: In reality, after the effect of tax loopholes,
or tax deductions, or tax incentives, corporations

00:09:07.600 --> 00:09:10.040
aren't paying a huge amount of tax.

00:09:10.040 --> 00:09:14.260
The corporate tax rate in the US is supposed
to be 21%.

00:09:14.260 --> 00:09:19.450
What you see is that in actuality, a lot of
corporations are probably paying a lot less

00:09:19.450 --> 00:09:20.560
than that.

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:25.750
And so then that feeds into the discourse
that they're not effectively paying a 21%

00:09:25.750 --> 00:09:26.750
tax.

00:09:26.750 --> 00:09:27.750
SIERRA: P.S.

00:09:27.750 --> 00:09:33.110
You may have noticed that the global minimum
tax of 15% is actually lower than the US’s

00:09:33.110 --> 00:09:35.600
current 21% tax rate.

00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:38.839
But a reminder that 15% is a floor.

00:09:38.839 --> 00:09:45.600
Plus, the 21% rate is often not fully paid,
and the effective rate - the rate companies

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:48.380
actually end up paying - is way lower.

00:09:48.380 --> 00:09:52.570
We’ll get into exactly how corporations
finegale this in a minute.

00:09:52.570 --> 00:09:59.350
But the key takeaway for now is that, in most
cases, a 15% floor would be higher than what

00:09:59.350 --> 00:10:04.970
actually gets paid, and it would force the
US to tighten our tax laws, otherwise other

00:10:04.970 --> 00:10:06.600
countries could claim the difference.

00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:10.640
OEI: Some of that tax minimization is legal
under the tax law, and that's a whole different

00:10:10.640 --> 00:10:11.640
conversation.

00:10:11.640 --> 00:10:15.790
But after the application of what one might
colloquially call loopholes, the effective

00:10:15.790 --> 00:10:18.610
rate might be in some circumstances reduced
to zero.

00:10:18.610 --> 00:10:22.800
And that is certainly something we've seen
among some corporations in the US.

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:28.540
SIERRA: To give you some examples - a US company
might reinvest in growth and development,

00:10:28.540 --> 00:10:32.890
this would lower their profit margin and they’d
have a lower tax liability.

00:10:32.890 --> 00:10:37.850
Another thing they can do is add solar panels,
this would give them a tax credit.

00:10:37.850 --> 00:10:42.990
These are just two tools to get tax breaks
in the US, among countless other tools that

00:10:42.990 --> 00:10:45.880
companies use to lower their tax bill.

00:10:45.880 --> 00:10:46.880
And it works.

00:10:46.880 --> 00:10:48.870
Let’s look at Amazon.

00:10:48.870 --> 00:10:53.000
This company has become an expert at working
the corporate tax system.

00:10:53.000 --> 00:10:59.310
You might remember this, back in 2018, Amazon
made a whole bunch of headlines…

00:10:59.310 --> 00:11:08.100
Fox Business: News out that Amazon is paying
zero in federal income taxes for the 2nd year

00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:09.240
in a row.

00:11:09.240 --> 00:11:12.660
Yahoo Finance: Zero, zero, zero dollars, zero
in taxes.

00:11:12.660 --> 00:11:18.310
CNBC: That massive company paid no federal
income tax on more than 11 billion in profits

00:11:18.310 --> 00:11:19.620
in 2018.

00:11:19.620 --> 00:11:26.080
SIERRA: And in following years, Amazon continued
to pay far less than the 21% rate.

00:11:26.080 --> 00:11:31.410
If the Global Minimum Tax became reality,
Amazon and other companies would not be able

00:11:31.410 --> 00:11:33.530
to reduce their tax bill unchecked.

00:11:33.530 --> 00:11:34.680
OEI: Yeah.

00:11:34.680 --> 00:11:40.589
So, Amazon is a good example because that's
a big US multinational corporation.

00:11:40.589 --> 00:11:45.779
And so, you'd look at that corporation and
you'd say, well, you are subject to a 21%

00:11:45.779 --> 00:11:51.410
rate in the US, but then the question is,
so in the US, how much is Amazon paying?

00:11:51.410 --> 00:11:55.950
And so the way ITEP, Institute for Taxation
and Economic Policy, right it’s a think

00:11:55.950 --> 00:12:00.520
tank, calculated the effective tax rate for
2021 was 6.1%.

00:12:00.520 --> 00:12:05.269
And looking back over years, 9.4% in 2020.

00:12:05.269 --> 00:12:10.279
That is based on a certain calculation, but
the bottom line is, you're looking at a 21%

00:12:10.279 --> 00:12:11.920
statutory rate.

00:12:11.920 --> 00:12:16.141
And then you are trying to back out and say,
how much of this income do we estimate as

00:12:16.141 --> 00:12:20.430
should be the taxable profit base, gee, it's
looking a lot less than 21%.

00:12:20.430 --> 00:12:24.209
SIERRA: I was going to say, six, nine, those
aren't 21.

00:12:24.209 --> 00:12:27.760
So, it's not even close.

00:12:27.760 --> 00:12:31.779
OEI: Yeah.

00:12:31.779 --> 00:12:38.149
SIERRA: For decades now, multinational companies
have been using all the tools we’ve talked

00:12:38.149 --> 00:12:42.190
about to lower their taxes, while making record
profits.

00:12:42.190 --> 00:12:50.300
In 2020, 55 of the largest corporations in
the US paid $0 in US federal corporate income

00:12:50.300 --> 00:12:54.740
tax, including FedEx, Nike, and Salesforce.

00:12:54.740 --> 00:13:03.269
And the kicker, that same year, together these
55 companies got $3.5 billion back from the

00:13:03.269 --> 00:13:05.570
government through rebates.

00:13:05.570 --> 00:13:10.870
But the biggest shock came from the Silicon
Six - Amazon, Facebook, Google’s parent

00:13:10.870 --> 00:13:18.740
company Alphabet, Netflix, Apple and Microsoft
who reportedly have a $100 billion tax gap

00:13:18.740 --> 00:13:20.900
that grew in the last decade.

00:13:20.900 --> 00:13:26.459
Yet, these companies still benefit from the
services that tax dollars bring to the societies

00:13:26.459 --> 00:13:32.720
they operate in, like the tax money used to
build schools, to pave roads, to provide healthcare,

00:13:32.720 --> 00:13:34.760
and to pay for a defense budget.

00:13:34.760 --> 00:13:40.690
The global minimum tax money might help us
pay for these exact needs or even allow governments

00:13:40.690 --> 00:13:45.810
to lower taxes on the individual side or decrease
the US deficit.

00:13:45.810 --> 00:13:48.870
At the end of the day it will be up to the
government.

00:13:48.870 --> 00:13:56.190
But ultimately, the global minimum tax would
bring in, collectively, $150 billion in additional

00:13:56.190 --> 00:13:58.040
tax revenue each year.

00:13:58.040 --> 00:14:01.430
RUBIN: The corporate income tax is a significant
revenue source.

00:14:01.430 --> 00:14:04.810
It brings in hundreds of billions of dollars
a year to the US government.

00:14:04.810 --> 00:14:09.650
So, as a US taxpayer, or as a taxpayer of
any country, you're going to want to care

00:14:09.650 --> 00:14:15.090
whether corporate income is taxed and how
it's taxed.

00:14:15.090 --> 00:14:20.029
It's also not just important as a dollar amount
of revenue source, but because corporations

00:14:20.029 --> 00:14:24.171
tend to be owned by higher income people,
the corporate income tax is one of the more

00:14:24.171 --> 00:14:27.040
progressive sources of revenue that the government
has.

00:14:27.040 --> 00:14:31.899
The idea is that you create a floor under
what companies pay and that provides money

00:14:31.899 --> 00:14:34.070
to governments to pay for programs.

00:14:34.070 --> 00:14:38.120
SIERRA: Does this apply to every company or
is it just the biggest ones?

00:14:38.120 --> 00:14:44.730
RUBIN: I think it will be tech companies,
pharmaceutical companies, probably some consumer

00:14:44.730 --> 00:14:49.360
product companies here and there, probably
some of the chip makers.

00:14:49.360 --> 00:14:53.330
And I think probably less so retailers and
finance industry companies.

00:14:53.330 --> 00:14:54.730
SIERRA: A lot of those new sectors.

00:14:54.730 --> 00:14:56.320
RUBIN: Right.

00:14:56.320 --> 00:15:05.480
SIERRA: And FYI, this only applies to corporations
making over 750 million Euros in annual revenue,

00:15:05.480 --> 00:15:08.079
that's roughly over $800 million dollars.

00:15:08.079 --> 00:15:12.329
OEI: I think the reason why people talk a
lot about tech is that often the customers,

00:15:12.329 --> 00:15:15.920
the digital users are in different jurisdictions
than the plant.

00:15:15.920 --> 00:15:20.570
And so, you know, the deal will be more salient
to those companies, but the terms of the deal

00:15:20.570 --> 00:15:22.959
don't name specific sectors.

00:15:22.959 --> 00:15:24.769
So it's literally by revenue.

00:15:24.769 --> 00:15:28.440
It's just that we are pretty sure that the
tech and the pharma company, those big companies

00:15:28.440 --> 00:15:29.959
will be hit by it.

00:15:29.959 --> 00:15:34.370
SIERRA: So, what if a country decides to sit
out?

00:15:34.370 --> 00:15:38.960
Won't that just attract multinational companies,
corporations to move there?

00:15:38.960 --> 00:15:39.960
RUBIN: Right.

00:15:39.960 --> 00:15:44.060
So, you need a critical mass of countries
in order to make this work.

00:15:44.060 --> 00:15:52.190
But the way it works is that it's imposed
by the country where the company is headquartered.

00:15:52.190 --> 00:15:57.940
So, if you're a US company, we will look at
how much profit you make in each country around

00:15:57.940 --> 00:16:03.520
the world and will charge you up to 15% to
make sure that you're not putting profits

00:16:03.520 --> 00:16:05.209
in any low tax country.

00:16:05.209 --> 00:16:10.870
So, if the US does that for its companies
and other countries that are part of this

00:16:10.870 --> 00:16:18.899
deal, France, the UK, Germany, Japan do the
same thing for companies that are headquartered

00:16:18.899 --> 00:16:24.390
in those places, all of a sudden you've got
a very significant chunk of global multinational

00:16:24.390 --> 00:16:28.139
income that's captured by this tax.

00:16:28.139 --> 00:16:30.720
SIERRA: So who wants this?

00:16:30.720 --> 00:16:32.899
Who's pushing hardest for it?

00:16:32.899 --> 00:16:35.959
RUBIN: The Biden Administration is pushing
very hard for this.

00:16:35.959 --> 00:16:40.610
And the Biden Administration wants to bring
everybody else along so that, as you were

00:16:40.610 --> 00:16:45.120
talking about, there aren't necessarily opportunities
for companies to go elsewhere and have an

00:16:45.120 --> 00:16:47.650
advantage if they're not a US company.

00:16:47.650 --> 00:16:52.000
Just as a policy matter, you want to minimize
the benefits that a company could get if it

00:16:52.000 --> 00:16:56.260
were to become a British company or an Australian
company or whatever.

00:16:56.260 --> 00:16:58.410
RUBIN: And other countries want this too.

00:16:58.410 --> 00:17:03.010
Other countries, like France and Germany,
there are lots of countries that are on board

00:17:03.010 --> 00:17:04.010
for this.

00:17:04.010 --> 00:17:07.620
OEI: The primary drivers of the whole thing
at the beginning were the OECD countries and

00:17:07.620 --> 00:17:09.520
then the G20 came on board.

00:17:09.520 --> 00:17:14.339
You know I think it's a complicated coalition
of stakeholders.

00:17:14.339 --> 00:17:18.891
Certainly the wealthier global North countries
I think are pushing it.

00:17:18.891 --> 00:17:20.939
So the EU is certainly there.

00:17:20.939 --> 00:17:24.069
In the Biden administration, the US is certainly
there.

00:17:24.069 --> 00:17:28.069
Although others have argued that the alignment
of interest is not completely perfect, right

00:17:28.069 --> 00:17:30.520
because the US has the big multinationals.

00:17:30.520 --> 00:17:34.540
And I think the US would like to be getting
more tax out of the multinationals, but we

00:17:34.540 --> 00:17:37.900
wouldn't want someone else to be getting the
tax instead of us, right so there's a bit

00:17:37.900 --> 00:17:39.140
of an interplay there.

00:17:39.140 --> 00:17:45.850
I think people would argue that it is also
good for non-haveney less wealthy countries,

00:17:45.850 --> 00:17:48.169
like lower income countries.

00:17:48.169 --> 00:17:52.660
This idea that if you put a 15% minimum on
the table, then you're less likely to have

00:17:52.660 --> 00:17:55.910
tax havens just basically eliminate the base.

00:17:55.910 --> 00:18:01.010
So less wealthy countries that may need to
raise revenue may also benefit.

00:18:01.010 --> 00:18:06.350
SIERRA: How do developing countries feel about
a global minimum tax?

00:18:06.350 --> 00:18:09.179
OEI: First of all, it's a very heterogeneous
group.

00:18:09.179 --> 00:18:14.840
And so you're talking from the BRICS, Brazil,
Russia, India, China, to smaller developing

00:18:14.840 --> 00:18:16.690
countries, lower income countries.

00:18:16.690 --> 00:18:18.789
There's a whole variety of actors here.

00:18:18.789 --> 00:18:20.990
I think views are mixed.

00:18:20.990 --> 00:18:22.060
Many have signed on.

00:18:22.060 --> 00:18:24.730
It's not entirely clear what's driving it.

00:18:24.730 --> 00:18:29.560
My own research sort of suggests that there
may be a wanting to comply in order to avoid

00:18:29.560 --> 00:18:32.740
being on some list of naughty actors, right…

00:18:32.740 --> 00:18:33.740
SIERRA: Interesting.

00:18:33.740 --> 00:18:34.740
Yeah.

00:18:34.740 --> 00:18:37.090
OEI: …that EU had put together right, but
it's very hard to know.

00:18:37.090 --> 00:18:41.760
Maybe the way to talk about it is I think
there is some anxiety about not getting an

00:18:41.760 --> 00:18:43.640
adequate cut of the deal.

00:18:43.640 --> 00:18:50.429
So, the worry is, are we going to be forced
to abandon some of our tax incentive regimes?

00:18:50.429 --> 00:18:52.210
That's a general tension with these rules.

00:18:52.210 --> 00:18:57.690
Are we specifying what the minimum tax is,
or are we actively telling countries how they

00:18:57.690 --> 00:18:59.669
can and cannot design their tax systems?

00:18:59.669 --> 00:19:00.669
SIERRA: Yeah.

00:19:00.669 --> 00:19:05.120
OEI: And there's some debate over how far
it goes and how far it should.

00:19:05.120 --> 00:19:10.570
SIERRA: So, I guess my question is where are
we at with all this?

00:19:10.570 --> 00:19:13.350
What's the current status of the global minimum
tax?

00:19:13.350 --> 00:19:15.140
RUBIN: It's messy right now.

00:19:15.140 --> 00:19:17.310
SIERRA: Sounds pretty messy.

00:19:17.310 --> 00:19:18.340
RUBIN: It's pretty messy.

00:19:18.340 --> 00:19:19.530
So, let's take it in two pieces.

00:19:19.530 --> 00:19:24.960
Abroad, countries are trying to start to go
ahead to put their pieces in place.

00:19:24.960 --> 00:19:28.460
France is leading the EU at the moment and
is trying to push this ahead to get European

00:19:28.460 --> 00:19:31.880
countries to agree on exactly how the system
will work and to implement it in the next

00:19:31.880 --> 00:19:33.030
year or so.

00:19:33.030 --> 00:19:38.280
SIERRA: Since we recorded our interviews,
Poland blocked a proposal that outlined how

00:19:38.280 --> 00:19:44.010
to implement the global minimum tax across
the EU, this is a major setback.

00:19:44.010 --> 00:19:47.890
But Europe is not the only place where progress
is stalled.

00:19:47.890 --> 00:19:54.030
RUBIN: In the US, it’s stalled in Congress
as part of the Build Back Better initiative.

00:19:54.030 --> 00:19:58.440
There's actually not a ton of controversy
among Democrats in Congress.

00:19:58.440 --> 00:20:03.830
Republicans don't like it, but Democrats are
basically fine with this piece of the legislation.

00:20:03.830 --> 00:20:06.880
It's the other parts of the bill that have
caused problems.

00:20:06.880 --> 00:20:12.159
So, if some reconciliation legislation, some
fiscal package moves in Congress, I would

00:20:12.159 --> 00:20:15.179
expect this to be part of it on the democratic
side.

00:20:15.179 --> 00:20:21.270
So, all of that, like the US minimum tax effort,
the international minimum tax effort, the

00:20:21.270 --> 00:20:25.049
idea is that everything sort of marches forward
together in coordination and you all know

00:20:25.049 --> 00:20:26.049
better than me…

00:20:26.049 --> 00:20:27.100
SIERRA: How's that going?

00:20:27.100 --> 00:20:32.429
RUBIN: The entire world marching forward together
in coordination doesn't always work very well.

00:20:32.429 --> 00:20:35.370
And so that, I think is, I'm not saying it
won't happen.

00:20:35.370 --> 00:20:39.540
I'm just saying it has been a long process
and will continue to be a long process with

00:20:39.540 --> 00:20:42.929
this sort of dance of US legislators looking
around the world and saying, "Are you guys

00:20:42.929 --> 00:20:43.929
really going to do this?"

00:20:43.929 --> 00:20:48.140
And then the European legislators looking
at Congress and saying, "Are you guys really

00:20:48.140 --> 00:20:49.140
going to do this?"

00:20:49.140 --> 00:20:50.140
Right?

00:20:50.140 --> 00:20:52.240
So, I think if everyone, you know, holds hands
and leaps, then it works.

00:20:52.240 --> 00:20:54.809
SIERRA: I was going to say, it's like getting
dared to jump into a pool or something.

00:20:54.809 --> 00:20:55.809
RUBIN: Yeah.

00:20:55.809 --> 00:20:56.809
SIERRA: Yeah.

00:20:56.809 --> 00:20:59.720
RUBIN: But if it starts being very clear that
it falls apart … So if for example, if we

00:20:59.720 --> 00:21:05.640
get to January 2023 and at least one chamber
of Congress is in Republican hands and this

00:21:05.640 --> 00:21:11.059
hasn't moved through Congress, European countries
are going to be very skeptical about the Biden

00:21:11.059 --> 00:21:12.560
Administration's ability to deliver on this.

00:21:12.560 --> 00:21:13.560
SIERRA: Okay.

00:21:13.560 --> 00:21:14.560
RUBIN: Yeah.

00:21:14.560 --> 00:21:15.560
So, it is you can't put that in the podcast.

00:21:15.560 --> 00:21:16.560
SIERRA: Oh, I can't put your shrug?

00:21:16.560 --> 00:21:18.279
RUBIN: You can't put my crazy shrug in my
podcast.

00:21:18.279 --> 00:21:20.640
But I've been covering several years and I've
just ...

00:21:20.640 --> 00:21:22.250
SIERRA: Crazy shrug.

00:21:22.250 --> 00:21:23.870
RUBIN: Crazy shrug.

00:21:23.870 --> 00:21:24.870
Yeah.

00:21:24.870 --> 00:21:30.020
SIERRA: There is an uphill battle ahead, and
what is at stake goes far beyond our government’s

00:21:30.020 --> 00:21:31.620
revenue stream.

00:21:31.620 --> 00:21:37.409
CODEPINK: Hey-He-Ho-Ho tax evaders have got
to go!

00:21:37.409 --> 00:21:39.809
Hey-He-Ho-Ho tax evaders have got to go!

00:21:39.809 --> 00:21:47.059
SIERRA: So closing out, we live in an atmosphere
of frustration with financial inequality and

00:21:47.059 --> 00:21:52.059
a lot of criticism of unfair advantages for
corporations, and of course, the super wealthy,

00:21:52.059 --> 00:21:55.740
a lot of people feel like it's a rigged game.

00:21:55.740 --> 00:22:01.240
Do you think that if this worked out and companies
were paying taxes more equally, and governments

00:22:01.240 --> 00:22:06.130
were gaining more revenue, that it might help
repair some of the trust that's been lost

00:22:06.130 --> 00:22:08.210
between citizens and corporations?

00:22:08.210 --> 00:22:09.400
OEI: Yeah.

00:22:09.400 --> 00:22:13.670
That's certainly the hope, I think the hope
is if you're seeing your biggest companies

00:22:13.670 --> 00:22:19.910
pay a larger share of their profits in taxes,
and you're less hearing about them hiding

00:22:19.910 --> 00:22:24.630
profits offshore, or putting them in low tax
jurisdictions instead of bringing them home,

00:22:24.630 --> 00:22:28.340
that contributes to a feeling of democratic
quality in the country.

00:22:28.340 --> 00:22:30.120
I think that's certainly the hope.

00:22:30.120 --> 00:22:33.150
Of course, now as a tax person, I have to
say, well, you have to worry about things

00:22:33.150 --> 00:22:38.520
like incidents, does the corporation pass
on the tax burden to shareholders or to workers?

00:22:38.520 --> 00:22:39.750
I don't know.

00:22:39.750 --> 00:22:40.750
Right.

00:22:40.750 --> 00:22:45.090
But certainly in terms of morale building,
I think a non-trivial part of the exercise

00:22:45.090 --> 00:22:47.539
of taxing is building citizenship.

00:22:47.539 --> 00:22:52.390
So this idea that wealthy companies and wealthy
individuals, right are paying a fair share

00:22:52.390 --> 00:23:01.860
I think certainly can't hurt.

